# Storybook

Colocate stories with the component: `ComponentName/ComponentName.tsx`, barrel `index.ts`, and `ComponentName.stories.tsx`. Follow existing files (`Button.stories.tsx`, `Input.stories.tsx`).

- Import `Meta` / `StoryObj` from `@storybook/react-vite`
- `title: 'UI/ComponentName'`, `satisfies Meta`, `layout: 'centered'`
- Named exports for variants (`Default`, `Placeholder`, `Disabled`)

Stories show the **component's own variants**, not each consumer. Inspect app usages to learn which props and states actually occur, then name stories generically.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — one story per call site
export const MailFolder: Story = {};
export const NoteEditorFont: Story = {};

// ✅ GOOD — the states the component actually has
export const Default: Story = {};
export const Placeholder: Story = { args: { value: undefined } };
export const Disabled: Story = { args: { disabled: true } };
```

Do not invent unused styling or prop combinations. The default canvas uses the inset token, so an inset control disappears on it. Paint the story background with the surface token. Do not wrap the story in `Card` or another surface just to show the fill. Follow `InlineSelect.stories.tsx`.

```tsx
parameters: {
  backgrounds: {
    options: {
      surface: { name: 'Surface', value: 'var(--color-surface)' },
    },
  },
},
globals: {
  backgrounds: { value: 'surface' },
},
```

For controlled inputs, keep value in local `useState`. Hide callback props in the controls table (`table: { disable: true }`). Do not change component APIs for stories.

Components that use TanStack `Link` / `useNavigate` wrap with `withRouter`. Compose extra decorators as an array (`[withRouter, …]`), do not call one decorator from another. PostCard uses `withPostCard` for that. Do not copy the app route tree into Storybook.

Do not call `useMatch({ from: '/_authenticated/...' })` in shared UI just to branch on the current page — pass a callback from the route. Storybook should not recreate app routes to satisfy that.
